{
  "note": "P, M and K Ganglion Cells",
  "source": "56e5e6fbeb73",
  "script": "a9927b00eb73",
  "voice": "Patrick - Engaging and Warm",
  "model": "eleven_v4",
  "fingerprint": "534eb9f867ac",
  "duration": 192.392,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "P, M and K Ganglion Cells",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "The axons of retinal ganglion cells form the optic nerve.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 3.84
        },
        {
          "text": "These cells fall into two major functional classes, the P cells and the M cells, with an additional third type known as the K ganglion cells.",
          "start": 4.08,
          "end": 13.12
        },
        {
          "text": "The classes differ in receptive field size, in conduction speed and in the kind of visual information they carry.",
          "start": 13.36,
          "end": 20.24
        },
        {
          "text": "These differences are the anatomical basis of the parallel pathways of vision.",
          "start": 20.36,
          "end": 24.8
        },
        {
          "text": "We'll meet each class in turn, and then look at how the shape of each cell fits its job.",
          "start": 24.84,
          "end": 29.52
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "P cells",
      "start": 30.38,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "First up are the P cells, which belong to the parvocellular division.",
          "start": 30.38,
          "end": 34.887
        },
        {
          "text": "They are the most numerous of the ganglion cell classes.",
          "start": 34.913,
          "end": 37.94
        },
        {
          "text": "Each one has a small receptive field, which means it samples a tiny patch of the retina with fine precision.",
          "start": 37.98,
          "end": 44.593
        },
        {
          "text": "P cells are colour sensitive, and they underlie high visual acuity and high spatial resolution.",
          "start": 44.647,
          "end": 50.487
        },
        {
          "text": "So the P cell stream carries the detail and the colour of the visual scene.",
          "start": 50.513,
          "end": 54.86
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "M cells",
      "start": 55.406,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Next come the M cells, which belong to the magnocellular division.",
          "start": 55.406,
          "end": 59.686
        },
        {
          "text": "Their receptive fields are large, pooling input from a wide patch of retina.",
          "start": 59.726,
          "end": 64.473
        },
        {
          "text": "They are fast conducting, and they are specialised for motion perception and rapidly changing detail, which gives them high temporal resolution.",
          "start": 64.499,
          "end": 72.526
        },
        {
          "text": "Where P cells resolve fine spatial detail, M cells resolve fine timing.",
          "start": 72.606,
          "end": 77.766
        },
        {
          "text": "In other words, the M cell stream tells the brain where things are moving and how fast the scene is changing.",
          "start": 77.806,
          "end": 84.326
        },
        {
          "text": "As for the class names, they come from the layers of the lateral geniculate nucleus that each type feeds.",
          "start": 84.366,
          "end": 90.406
        },
        {
          "text": "M cells project to the large celled, or magnocellular, layers of this nucleus, whereas P cells project to its small celled, or parvocellular, layers.",
          "start": 90.446,
          "end": 99.539
        },
        {
          "text": "Each division therefore names the pathway the cell drives, not the cell itself.",
          "start": 99.593,
          "end": 104.446
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "K ganglion cells",
      "start": 105.221,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Beyond these two major classes, K ganglion cells are an additional type, identified as transmitting aspects of colour.",
          "start": 105.221,
          "end": 112.861
        },
        {
          "text": "As noted in class, they carry blue light information.",
          "start": 112.901,
          "end": 116.394
        },
        {
          "text": "This complements the colour sensitivity of the P cell stream.",
          "start": 116.448,
          "end": 120.021
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Morphology of the three classes",
      "start": 120.79,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Turning now to shape, the P ganglion cell has a small, compact dendritic tree, which matches its small receptive field.",
          "start": 120.79,
          "end": 128.363
        },
        {
          "text": "The M ganglion cell, by contrast, has a much larger, widely branching tree, matching its large receptive field.",
          "start": 128.417,
          "end": 135.403
        },
        {
          "text": "The K ganglion cell sits between the two in size, with a sparse and irregular pattern of branching.",
          "start": 135.457,
          "end": 141.31
        },
        {
          "text": "In this way, the functional contrast between the classes is visible in the anatomy itself.",
          "start": 141.35,
          "end": 146.603
        },
        {
          "text": "Put simply, dendritic tree size sets receptive field size, and that in turn sets what each class is good at detecting.",
          "start": 146.657,
          "end": 154.07
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Summary",
      "start": 154.828,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "To sum up, ganglion cell axons form the optic nerve and belong to two major classes.",
          "start": 154.828,
          "end": 160.801
        },
        {
          "text": "P cells of the parvocellular division are numerous, with small receptive fields and colour sensitivity, along with high visual acuity and high spatial resolution.",
          "start": 160.855,
          "end": 170.788
        },
        {
          "text": "M cells of the magnocellular division have large receptive fields and conduct fast.",
          "start": 170.828,
          "end": 176.001
        },
        {
          "text": "They handle motion perception and rapidly changing detail, with high temporal resolution.",
          "start": 176.055,
          "end": 181.428
        },
        {
          "text": "Lastly, K ganglion cells are an additional type that transmits aspects of colour, notably blue light information.",
          "start": 181.468,
          "end": 188.641
        },
        {
          "text": "And that rounds off the three classes of retinal ganglion cell.",
          "start": 188.695,
          "end": 192.428
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
