    {"duration":49.81551,"alignment":{"characters":"Next come the M cells, which belong to the magnocellular division. Their receptive fields are large, pooling input from a wide patch of retina. They are fast conducting, and they are specialised for motion perception and rapidly changing detail, which gives them high temporal resolution. Where P cells resolve fine spatial detail, M cells resolve fine timing. In other words, the M cell stream tells the brain where things are moving and how fast the scene is changing. As for the class names, they come from the layers of the lateral geniculate nucleus that each type feeds. M cells project to the large celled, or magnocellular, layers of this nucleus, whereas P cells project to its small celled, or parvocellular, layers. Each division therefore names the pathway the cell drives, not the cell itself.","starts":[0,80,160,240,320,400,440,480,520,560,680,720,760,800,960,1040,1120,1200,1280,1360,1440,1520,1680,1760,1776,1792,1808,1824,1840,1920,1947,1973,2000,2053,2107,2160,2200,2220,2240,2320,2347,2373,2400,2440,2480,2560,2640,2720,2800,2880,2926,2971,3017,3063,3109,3154,3200,3280,3400,3520,3640,3760,3880,4000,4120,4240,4280,4320,4352,4384,4416,4448,4480,4560,4600,4640,4686,4731,4777,4823,4869,4914,4960,5040,5080,5120,5160,5200,5240,5280,5360,5413,5467,5520,5600,5728,5856,5984,6112,6240,6280,6320,6360,6400,6440,6480,6533,6587,6640,6720,6768,6816,6864,6912,6960,7040,7060,7080,7100,7120,7360,7467,7573,7600,7627,7653,7680,7760,7808,7856,7904,7952,8000,8080,8120,8160,8240,8267,8293,8320,8560,8800,9040,9067,9093,9100,9107,9113,9120,9240,9280,9320,9360,9440,9500,9560,9620,9680,9920,9954,9989,10023,10057,10091,10126,10160,10240,10320,10400,10427,10453,10462,10471,10480,10520,10530,10540,10550,10560,10640,10667,10693,10720,10800,10846,10891,10937,10983,11029,11074,11120,11180,11240,11300,11360,11480,11520,11560,11600,11760,11787,11813,11840,11867,11893,11920,12000,12027,12053,12080,12149,12217,12286,12354,12423,12491,12560,12680,1